          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies differ about delayed prescribing for respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "**Yes, bodies differ.** The guidance in this corpus describes a split on how strongly delayed prescribing should be encouraged.\n\n- **Supportive view:** Some guidance describes delayed prescribing as a recommended tool for reducing immediate prescriptions. Under this approach, the patient gets a prescription to fill only if symptoms fail to improve or get worse by a stated point. It is described as a middle option that cuts antibiotic use substantially while keeping patients satisfied. It is most useful when the clinician judges a bacterial infection unlikely but the patient's expectations or limited access to follow-up make a plain refusal hard. (Source: *Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated*.)\n- **Cautious view:** Other guidance is wary. It points out that delayed prescribing still leads to a prescription in a substantial fraction of cases. It also argues that it may reinforce the belief that these illnesses need antibiotics. (Same source.)\n- **Sore throat:** Some national guidance puts more weight on clinical scoring and less on testing every patient before treatment. (Same source.)\n- **Related disagreement in urinary infection:** Some European guidance offers delayed or no antibiotics for mild cystitis as a shared decision. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default. The disagreement is about how much antibiotic exposure is worth avoiding, not about the facts. (*Uncomplicated urinary tract infection*. This is not a respiratory infection, so it is included only for context.)\n\n**What the corpus does not answer:** I could not find passages on how well delayed prescribing reduces antibiotic use, shortens symptoms, or affects satisfaction. I also found nothing on how often patients fill the prescription early or never fill it. So I cannot say how strong the evidence is for either side. A clinician or the original studies would be the place to check those points.\n\n**A general safety point:** The general cold reference says that symptoms getting worse after an early improvement, or fever returning, are reasons to seek further assessment. If someone has severe breathing difficulty, confusion, or signs of sepsis such as a racing heartbeat or feeling very unwell, they should seek urgent care now rather than wait.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
